<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gazi II Giray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1554–1607</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gazi II Giray was khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> from 1588 to 1607, with a brief interruption in 1596. He led Crimean forces in the Ottoman wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-turkish-war">Long Turkish War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and raided Muscovy, reaching the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1591. Writing under the pen name Gazayi, he was a noted poet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gazi-ii-giray.jpg" alt="Gazi II Giray" width="320" height="547" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1598 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gazy_II_Giray,_standing._Secaatname,_1598.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
